# ===========================================================================
# Line endings — normalised, so an editor cannot manufacture a change
# ===========================================================================
# Written on 25 August 2026 after a phantom conflict cost most of an
# afternoon. A branch appeared to have rewritten src/pages/educator/Messages.tsx
# — every line deleted and re-added, byte-identical, CRLF instead of LF. It had
# changed no code at all. That was harmless until somebody edited the same file
# for real, at which point git had two versions of it and no way to know that
# one of them was not a change. The pull request blocked on a conflict nobody
# had actually caused.
#
# This is not a Windows problem or a Mac problem, it is a problem with having
# both. Three of us are on Windows and one is on a Mac, and every editor has an
# opinion about how a line ends.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text and what is binary, and stores every
# text file with LF regardless of what the machine that committed it uses.
# `eol=lf` checks them back out as LF too, rather than converting on the way
# out — one representation everywhere, so a diff only ever shows real edits.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Images. `text=auto` already detects these, but saying so costs nothing and
# makes the intent explicit for anyone adding a new asset type below.
*.png binary
*.svg text eol=lf

# ===========================================================================
# NOTE FOR WHOEVER MERGES THIS
# ===========================================================================
# No re-normalisation is included on purpose. `git add --renormalize .` would
# rewrite every tracked file in one commit and collide with every open branch.
# Files normalise as they are next touched, which is slower and disturbs
# nobody. If a mass renormalise is ever wanted, do it when no pull request is
# open and by itself.
